<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">António Vieira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1608">1608</a>–<a href="/years/1697">1697</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">António Vieira (1608–1697) was a Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary, diplomat and preacher, considered a master of classical Portuguese prose. Raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>, he served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> IV as an adviser and envoy after 1640, and from 1653 led the Jesuit missions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a> and Grão-Pará, where he campaigned against the enslavement of indigenous people until the settlers expelled the Jesuits in 1661. He was later tried by the Inquisition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-vieira.jpg" alt="António Vieira" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Padre_Ant%C3%B3nio_Vieira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>António Vieira was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> on 6 February 1608, the son of Cristóvão Vieira Ravasco, whose mother Maria de Azevedo was a mulatto woman. In 1614 he went with his parents to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, where his father was posted as a registrar, and was educated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>. He entered the Jesuit novitiate in 1625 and pronounced his first vows two years later. At eighteen he was teaching rhetoric, and later dogmatic theology, at the college of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olinda">Olinda</a>. He was ordained a priest in 1635, and his three patriotic sermons at Bahia from 1638 to 1640 gave him a name as an orator.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the revolution of 1640 placed John IV on the throne, Vieira went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> to congratulate the new king, who made him tutor to the Infante Dom Pedro, royal preacher and a member of the Royal Council. He worked for commerce and a national bank, and in pamphlets denounced a society that discriminated against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-christians">New Christians</a> and called for reform of the Inquisition. From 1647 he was a diplomat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, urging in his Papel Forte that Pernambuco be ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> as the price of peace.</p><p>Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a> in early 1653, he fulfilled a youthful vow to work for the conversion of African slaves and native Indians. In April 1655 he obtained royal decrees that put the missions under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, with himself as superior, and forbade the enslavement of natives except in specified cases. In August 1659 he signed the Treaty of the Mapuá with indigenous groups on the Marajó Archipelago.</p><p>Colonists and rival clergy opposed him, and in 1661, after a popular revolt, he and thirty-one other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> were sent back to Portugal. His belief in the prophecies of the shoemaker poet Bandarra, and in a coming Fifth Empire, led the Inquisition to imprison him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> from October 1665 to December 1667 and forbid him to teach, write or preach.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Vieira regained his freedom on the accession of Pedro II and spent six years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where Pope Clement X invited him to preach before the cardinals and he became confessor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christina-queen-of-sweden">Queen Christina</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. His report on the Inquisition led Pope Innocent XI to suspend it in Portugal for seven years, from 1674 to 1681. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in January 1681, became superior of the province in 1687, and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>, on 18 July 1697. His works include fifteen volumes of Sermões and the Cartas, and the Complete Works began publication in 2013.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “António Vieira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jesuit <b>António Vieira</b> campaigned against the enslavement of indigenous people.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-maranhao">State of Maranhão</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Freguesia da Sé, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in Salvador, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.13&amp;year=1665" aria-label="Freguesia da Sé on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of António Vieira"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4393 4234h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4393 4234h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.13&amp;year=1665">What was here in 1665? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
